 E004 NVIDIA has also recently been negotiating to provide financial guarantees for OpenAI’s lease of a large data center in Ohio {"who": "NVIDIA (per The Information)", "what": "negotiating to guarantee OpenAI's lease of a large Ohio data center", "how_much": "the data center could cost as much as $500 billion if fully built (asset cost, not the guarantee amount)", "status": "rumour"}
A1 On July 1, according to the technology news outlet The Information, NVIDIA is providing financial guarantees to young cloud service providers that lease or rent its GPUs—if these companies fail to secure enough AI developers to rent their computing capacity, NVIDIA will repurchase their unsold GPU capacity at a pre-agreed price. | In return, NVIDIA will receive a percentage of these cloud providers’ revenue, with the revenue-sharing rate gradually declining over the term of the contract. GPU cloud providers Firmus and Sharon AI have already joined the program, and three executives with existing business ties to NVIDIA confirmed the arrangement. | On July 1, NVIDIA published a statement on its official website announcing a new business model that combines revenue sharing with credit support, enabling AI cloud providers to procure NVIDIA infrastructure without bearing the full upfront capital expenditure, and allowing them to offer computing services to downstream AI-native enterprises, model developers, and corporate clients. | A NVIDIA spokesperson also confirmed the existence of the program. | According to NVIDIA’s official press release, the company will earn not only standard product revenue but also an additional share of cloud service revenue, creating a usage-based, recurring revenue stream. | Sharon AI and Firmus are the first cloud providers participating in this model. Sharon AI plans to deploy up to 40,000 NVIDIA Grace Blackwell GB300 GPUs, while Firmus is constructing a DSX AI Factory campus in Batam, Indonesia, expected to scale up to 360 megawatts and accommodate up to 170,000 NVIDIA GPUs. | A data center executive commented on such NVIDIA deals as “killing two birds with one stone.” He explained that if NVIDIA merely provided a guarantee for a data center facility lease, “you’d still face the question of ‘how to finance the GPUs’”; but if NVIDIA commits to purchasing any unsold compute capacity within the facility, “the GPU financing issue is resolved—and so is the data center’s financing problem.” | In September 2024, NVIDIA committed to purchasing all of CoreWeave’s unsold capacity through 2032 if CoreWeave failed to secure tenants, under a contract then valued at $6.3 billion. | According to a regulatory filing submitted by NVIDIA in May of this year (covering the quarter ended in April), NVIDIA subsequently added another $3.5 billion to guarantee customer data center leases in exchange for rights to purchase their shares.
A2 On July 1, according to the technology news outlet The Information, NVIDIA is providing financial guarantees to young cloud service providers that lease or rent its GPUs—if these companies fail to secure enough AI developers to rent their computing capacity, NVIDIA will repurchase their unsold GPU capacity at a pre-agreed price. | GPU cloud providers Firmus and Sharon AI have already joined the program, and three executives with existing business ties to NVIDIA confirmed the arrangement. | On July 1, NVIDIA published a statement on its official website announcing a new business model that combines revenue sharing with credit support, enabling AI cloud providers to procure NVIDIA infrastructure without bearing the full upfront capital expenditure, and allowing them to offer computing services to downstream AI-native enterprises, model developers, and corporate clients. | According to the report, the initiative is internally referred to by some NVIDIA personnel as the 'AI Compute Partnership.' | According to prior reporting by The Information, NVIDIA has also recently been negotiating to provide financial guarantees for OpenAI’s lease of a large data center in Ohio—an asset that, if fully built out at current prices for chips, labor, power, and other materials, could cost as much as $500 billion. | In September 2024, NVIDIA committed to purchasing all of CoreWeave’s unsold capacity through 2032 if CoreWeave failed to secure tenants, under a contract then valued at $6.3 billion. | According to a regulatory filing submitted by NVIDIA in May of this year (covering the quarter ended in April), NVIDIA subsequently added another $3.5 billion to guarantee customer data center leases in exchange for rights to purchase their shares.
