 E044 Sharon AI, deploying up to 40,000 Grace Blackwell GB300 GPUs across new capacity in Australia
A1,A2,A3,B2,B3 On July 1, 2026, NVIDIA introduced a business model that lets AI cloud operators deploy large-scale, multi-tenant AI factories through a combination of revenue sharing and credit support, with the resulting facilities built as DSX AI factories.
A1,A3,B3 Sharon AI, deploying up to 40,000 Grace Blackwell GB300 GPUs across new capacity in Australia, and Firmus, building a 360-megawatt DSX-aligned AI factory campus in Batam, Indonesia, targeting up to 170,000 GPUs, are among the first partners.
A1,B1 Firmus’s $25–30 billion in committed off-take converts into recognized revenue on schedule.
A2,A3,B2 Participating clouds procure NVIDIA infrastructure and sell NVIDIA-powered services; NVIDIA earns its standard product revenue plus a share of the cloud revenue generated on the supported capacity.
A2,B1 Sharon AI, deploying up to 40,000 Grace Blackwell GB300 GPUs across new capacity in Australia
A2,B1 Firmus, building a 360-megawatt DSX-aligned AI factory campus in Batam, Indonesia, targeting up to 170,000 GPUs
A2 Introduced as a platform at GTC Taipei and now the design standard for the AI factories that Sharon AI and Firmus are building under the July 1 financing model
A2,B3 Firmus is explicitly building a DSX-aligned campus in Batam, co-designing its HyperCube architecture to NVIDIA’s DSX blueprints, and states the integration is meant to improve tokens per watt and reduce cost per token for its customers.
A2 Whether Firmus’s $25–30 billion in committed off-take converts into recognized revenue on schedule.
A3 Introduced as a platform at GTC Taipei and now the design standard for the AI factories that Sharon AI and Firmus are building under the July 1 financing model, DSX reframes the gigawatt from a power budget into a revenue function.
A3 The layer that matters most for revenue per gigawatt is MaxLPS, which combines liquid cooling with software-driven in-rack power tuning to run up to 40% more GPUs at their most efficient operating point inside a fixed power budget, on NVIDIA’s figures.
A3,B2 NVIDIA has partly funded its own demand through investments in OpenAI, CoreWeave, and Nebius, and the revenue-share model extends that pattern by other means.
B1 On July 1, 2026, NVIDIA introduced a business model that lets AI cloud operators deploy large-scale, multi-tenant AI factories through a combination of revenue sharing and credit support

